        <h1>My first open-source PR was merged</h1>
        <ul class="journal-tags" aria-label="Post hashtags">
          <li>#OpenSource</li><li>#C</li><li>#Learning</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="journal-article-body">
          <p>My first pull request to an open-source project has been merged. It went into <strong>codetrial</strong>, an open-source project maintained by Jserv, a professor in National Cheng Kung University’s Department of Computer Science and Information Engineering.</p>
          <h2>What I worked on</h2>
          <p>The issue was that test results involving special floating-point values—positive infinity, negative infinity, or NaN—could produce invalid JSON and cause the harness to lose its results. I updated the C output helper to represent non-finite values as JSON <code>null</code>, for both individual doubles and arrays of doubles, and added regression cases for those values.</p>
          <p>It was a focused fix, but it taught me to trace a bug from a report through the output code and tests, then check that the change handles both the simple and mixed cases.</p>
          <h2>A first step</h2>
          <p>I’ve used open-source software for a long time, so seeing my own contribution accepted felt like a meaningful first step toward giving back. It made contributing feel more tangible and encouraged me to keep learning by building, reading other people’s code, and sharing improvements.</p>
